Here's what was created in `ErrorBoundary.tsx`:

**Component API**

```tsx
<ErrorBoundary
  onError={(error, errorInfo) => sendToSentry(error, errorInfo)} // optional
  fallback={<MyCustomFallback />}                                  // optional
>
  <YourApp />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

**Key design decisions:**

| Detail | Choice |
|---|---|
| Class component | Required — React error boundaries only work in class components via `getDerivedStateFromError` / `componentDidCatch` |
| Retry | Resets `hasError` state, causing the subtree to re-render from scratch |
| Logging | Structured `console.error` with message, stack, component stack, and timestamp; forwarded to optional `onError` prop for external services (Sentry, Datadog, etc.) |
| Custom fallback | Pass any `ReactNode` via `fallback` prop to fully replace the default UI |
| Stack trace | Collapsible `<details>` element — visible for debugging, not intrusive for users |

**Usage example:**

```tsx
// wrap at the app root
<ErrorBoundary onError={(e, info) => Sentry.captureException(e, { extra: info })}>
  <App />
</ErrorBoundary>

// or scope to a single section
<ErrorBoundary fallback={<p>Widget failed to load.</p>}>
  <Widget />
</ErrorBoundary>
```
